    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header">
                <span class="lesson-number">Lesson 1</span>
                <h1>Introduction to ASP.NET Core</h1>
                <div class="lesson-meta">
                    <span class="lesson-duration">
                        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                            <polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/>
                        </svg>
                        25 min
                    </span>
                    <span class="lesson-level intermediate">Intermediate</span>
                </div>
            </header>

            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Welcome to ASP.NET Core Web APIs!</h2>
                    <p>In this course, you'll learn how to build production-ready REST APIs using ASP.NET Core. By the end, you'll be able to create secure, scalable, and well-documented APIs that follow best practices.</p>
                    
                    <p>This first lesson introduces ASP.NET Core, its architecture, and how it differs from other web frameworks.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is ASP.NET Core?</h2>
                    <p>ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform, high-performance, open-source framework for building modern, cloud-enabled, Internet-connected applications. It's the successor to ASP.NET and was redesigned from the ground up.</p>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Key Features</span>
                        </div>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Cross-platform:</strong> Runs on Windows, Linux, and macOS</li>
                            <li><strong>High performance:</strong> One of the fastest web frameworks available</li>
                            <li><strong>Modular:</strong> Pay for what you use with NuGet packages</li>
                            <li><strong>Unified:</strong> Single framework for web UI and APIs</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>ASP.NET Core vs ASP.NET Framework</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>ASP.NET Core (Modern)</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Cross-platform</li>
                                <li>Open source</li>
                                <li>High performance</li>
                                <li>Built-in dependency injection</li>
                                <li>Unified MVC and Web API</li>
                            </ul>
                            <span class="tag recommended">Use This</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>ASP.NET Framework (Legacy)</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Windows only</li>
                                <li>Closed source (mostly)</li>
                                <li>Heavier runtime</li>
                                <li>Separate MVC and Web API</li>
                                <li>Maintenance mode</li>
                            </ul>
                            <span class="tag legacy">Legacy</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Request Pipeline</h2>
                    <p>ASP.NET Core uses a middleware pipeline to handle HTTP requests. Each middleware component can process the request, pass it to the next component, or short-circuit the pipeline.</p>

                    <div class="pipeline-diagram">
                        <div class="pipeline-step">Request</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-step">Logging</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-step">Auth</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-step">Routing</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="pipeline-step">Endpoint</div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

// Middleware is added in order
app.UseHttpsRedirection();   // 1. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
app.UseAuthentication();     // 2. Who is the user?
app.UseAuthorization();      // 3. Can they access this?
app.MapControllers();        // 4. Route to controller

app.Run();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Project Structure</h2>
                    <p>A typical ASP.NET Core Web API project structure:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">MyApi/
├── Controllers/           # API endpoints
│   └── WeatherController.cs
├── Models/               # Data models/DTOs
│   └── WeatherForecast.cs
├── Services/             # Business logic
│   └── WeatherService.cs
├── Data/                 # Database context
│   └── AppDbContext.cs
├── Program.cs            # Application entry point
├── appsettings.json      # Configuration
└── MyApi.csproj          # Project file</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Program.cs File</h2>
                    <p>Modern ASP.NET Core uses a simplified Program.cs with top-level statements:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add services to the container
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();
builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();

var app = builder.Build();

// Configure the HTTP request pipeline
if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseSwagger();
    app.UseSwaggerUI();
}

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();</code></pre>

                    <h3>Two Phases</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Builder phase:</strong> Configure services and dependencies</li>
                        <li><strong>App phase:</strong> Configure the request pipeline (middleware)</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>REST API Principles</h2>
                    <p>We'll be building RESTful APIs. REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style with these principles:</p>

                    <div class="rest-methods">
                        <div class="method-card get">
                            <span class="method">GET</span>
                            <span class="desc">Retrieve resources</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="method-card post">
                            <span class="method">POST</span>
                            <span class="desc">Create new resources</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="method-card put">
                            <span class="method">PUT</span>
                            <span class="desc">Update entire resources</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="method-card patch">
                            <span class="method">PATCH</span>
                            <span class="desc">Partial updates</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="method-card delete">
                            <span class="method">DELETE</span>
                            <span class="desc">Remove resources</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># RESTful URL patterns
GET    /api/products         # Get all products
GET    /api/products/5       # Get product with ID 5
POST   /api/products         # Create a new product
PUT    /api/products/5       # Update product 5
DELETE /api/products/5       # Delete product 5</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>HTTP Status Codes</h2>
                    <p>APIs communicate using HTTP status codes:</p>

                    <div class="status-codes">
                        <div class="code-group success">
                            <h4>2xx Success</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>200</code> OK</li>
                                <li><code>201</code> Created</li>
                                <li><code>204</code> No Content</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-group client-error">
                            <h4>4xx Client Error</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>400</code> Bad Request</li>
                                <li><code>401</code> Unauthorized</li>
                                <li><code>403</code> Forbidden</li>
                                <li><code>404</code> Not Found</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-group server-error">
                            <h4>5xx Server Error</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>500</code> Internal Server Error</li>
                                <li><code>503</code> Service Unavailable</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform, high-performance web framework.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>The request pipeline is built from middleware components.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Program.cs has two phases: service configuration and pipeline configuration.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>REST APIs use HTTP methods and status codes for communication.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>

